CRITERION (b) every step holds as written -- FAIL, on step 7 (secret hygiene) only; every other attack below was refuted
 Step 7 ("Every occurrence in events.jsonl, proxy.log or out/ is replaced by [REDACTED] before the script exits, on every path";
 "any secret appearing in last.json -> skip secret-leak"):
 F1a. Attack: a file in out/ the harness cannot open O_RDWR. `redact_path` swallows OSError (tools.py: `except OSError: return`) and
      os.walk swallows scandir errors, so redaction silently skips them (fail-OPEN).
      Repro (end-to-end, real container, stub creates the files, host uid == container uid 1000):
        stub: cp /codex-home/auth.json /out/auth-copy.json ; echo $SECRET > /out/readonly.txt; chmod 444 /out/readonly.txt ;
              mkdir /out/locked; echo $SECRET > /out/locked/inner.txt; chmod 000 /out/locked
        `SWARM_DIR=.. SWARM_TREE=.. CODEX_BIN=stub CODEX_AUTH=fake nice -n 10 flock $LOCK bash swarm/codex-check.sh T 1` -> rc=0, verdict written.
        After exit: `auth-copy.json` was redacted (control) but `out/readonly.txt` and `out/locked/inner.txt` STILL CONTAIN the secret
        (grep -rl after chmod -R u+rwX). Docker-free reproduction: `tools.py secrets redact auth events.jsonl proxy.log out` on
        0444 / 0400 / dir-000 files -> exit ok, secret remains in all three.
      Note the harness already knows this hazard (it does `chmod -R u+rwX "$AUD/out"` at the START of the next run and on $TMP at exit)
      but not before redacting. Remedy verified: `chmod -R u+rwX out` before `secrets redact` -> no residual secret.
 F1b. Attack: the auth file is bind-mounted RW (brief step 4) and the secret list is read only AFTER the run (cleanup and step 7 both
      call load_secrets on $CODEX_AUTH at that time). If the container rewrites or truncates the file (a token refresh does the first),
      the values Codex was actually given are no longer "in $CODEX_AUTH" and are neither leak-checked nor redacted.
      Repro A (stub echoes auth.json into events + out/ files, then rewrites auth.json in place): events.jsonl (twice) and out/.hidden,
        out/sub/deep.txt kept the OLD secret; only the new value would be redacted.
      Repro B (stub puts the secret in last.json.result, then `: > /codex-home/auth.json`): the harness wrote a `VERDICT: PASS`
        file whose `result:` line is the full secret into .swarm/verdicts/, no secret-leak skip, and the host auth file is left at 0 bytes.
      Remedy verified: redacting run3's leftovers with the ORIGINAL auth file removes the secret from events.jsonl and out/ files;
      taking the secret set from a pre-run snapshot (union with post-run) would have made Repro B a `secret-leak` skip.
      (Filename-embedded secrets, e.g. `out/tok_FAKE...name`, are also not redacted -- lesser.)
      Judgement: F1a is a plain counterexample to a universal statement in the brief; F1b is letter-compliant only if "$CODEX_AUTH" is read
      at the end, which defeats the purpose stated in ruling CD-e (public archive). Both are fixable inside CD1's territory
      (snapshot secrets before `compose run`; chmod u+rwX before redacting; treat a file that still cannot be rewritten as a failure).
      Neither is covered by the worker's smoketest or the oracle (no read-only-file, no auth-rotation case).
